    "title_en": "Measurements of the total and differential inclusive electron neutrino charged-current cross sections on argon with SBND",
    "abstract_en": "This work presents measurements of the total and single-differential electron neutrino charged-current cross sections on argon using the Short-Baseline Near Detector (SBND) at Fermilab. We present the highest statistics inclusive $ν_e + \\bar ν_e$ (95.2% $ν_e$ and 4.8% $\\barν_e$) charged-current cross section measurement on argon to date and the first inclusive $ν_e + \\bar ν_e$ cross section measurement using the Booster Neutrino Beam. Direct measurements of this channel are crucial for future studies of both long- and short-baseline neutrino oscillations through electron neutrino appearance, which in turn are vital for answering open questions in neutrino phenomenology and searching for beyond the Standard Model physics at the intensity frontier. The data used for this work were collected with an exposure of $8.81 \\times10^{19}$ protons on target, and 729 candidate events are selected with a minimum electron energy of 500 MeV. The single-differential cross sections are extracted in electron energy and angle with respect to the beam axis. The flux-averaged total cross section is measured to be $σ= [3.90 \\pm 0.18\\,\\text{(stat)} \\pm 0.41\\,\\text{(syst)}] \\times 10^{-39}$ cm$^2$/nucleon for a predicted average neutrino energy of $\\langle E_ν\\rangle=1.8$ GeV for selected events. The total and differential cross sections are compared to predictions from common neutrino event generators. The data show a preference for generators that predict a lower overall cross section.",
